+
+## 2026-09-19 (later still): CORRECTION — there are two defects, not one
+
+The entry above concluded that "quantized KV + FA is broken at head_dim 128". That
+was wrong, and the way it was wrong is worth recording.
+
+All the head_dim 256 evidence had been taken on the promoted **llama.cpp** runtime,
+never on PrismML's own build. Running the pristine PrismML build (build 1, commit
+9a9394a, no TurboQuant code at all) on SC117 — head_dim **256**, the head size that
+was supposed to be the good case — gives:
+
+| KV | PPL at head_dim 256 | |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| f16 | 6.9921 | fine |
+| **q8_0** | **92.2627** | broken |
+| q4_0 | 7.0140 | fine |
+
+So q8_0 KV is broken on PrismML's base **at every head size** (329.4764 at 128,
+92.2627 at 256). The 329 in the earlier table was never a head_dim effect; it was
+PrismML's own bug, and it happened to sit next to a real head_dim effect.
+
+The corrected picture, two independent defects:
+
+**A — PrismML's base breaks q8_0 KV, at any head size.** Reproduces on the pristine
+build, so the TurboQuant port is not involved. q4_0 is fine, and their own KV work
+is scoped to Q4_0 by construction (the mean-centering feature errors out on any K
+type other than Q4_0, and the docs say Q4_0 only) — a fork that only ever validated
+the Q4_0 path is exactly what "q4_0 works, q8_0 does not" looks like. Their 93 own
+commits touched `llama-kv-cache.cpp` (2), `llama-context.cpp` (7) and
+`llama-graph.cpp` (8), and **no FA file at all**, so the kernels are upstream's.
+
+**B — TurboQuant KV breaks at head_dim 128.** turbo3 8038.7921 at 128 vs 7.1292 at
+256; turbo4 4064.1819 vs 7.0756. Byte-identical per-chunk values on both branches,
+so it is one shared code path, and on the llama.cpp base q8_0 at head_dim 128 is a
+working control (10.7160). This one is real and it is TurboQuant's.
+
+### How the earlier framing went wrong
+
+The original table was read as "f16 fine, every quantized type broken → it must be
+about head size". That is only valid if the quantized types share a cause, and they
+did not: one of them was PrismML's q8_0 bug and the other was the TurboQuant
+head_dim bug. The cheap test that would have caught it — PrismML's own build with
+q8_0 KV on a head_dim 256 model — was not run, because the head_dim 256 numbers had
+already been taken on the other runtime. **Cross-runtime comparisons need the same
+control run on both runtimes.**
+
+### Op-level localisation, kept because it still holds
+
+`test-backend-ops` (built here with tests ON) on this build:
+
+```text
+FLASH_ATTN_EXT  -> Backend ROCm0: FAIL, 102 cases
+                   by hsk: 72 -> 98, 256 -> 3, 64 -> 1      hsk 128 -> 0
+SET_ROWS        -> Backend ROCm0: OK
+```
+
+Two things follow. The hsk=72 cluster is the non-128 head padding gap the port map
+already tracks as open, not this defect. And **hsk=128 has zero FLASH_ATTN_EXT
+failures**, so at the op level the attention kernel is numerically right there —
+which puts defect B at the graph / cache level rather than in the kernel, and rules
+the kernel out as the place to look first.
+
+One trap worth recording: the first run of that instrument reported
+`failed to initialize ROCm: no ROCm-capable device is detected` and silently tested
+only the CPU backend, because the script put ROCm's `bin` ahead of the compat
+runtime on PATH. The compat directory has to come first.
